  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The 6 keys to a great home are functionality, comfort, personal expression, natural light, connection to nature, and spaces that bring people together. A house that gets all six right works smoothly on a busy Tuesday and feels like a retreat on a Saturday. If you are building, the best time to plan for them is before your floor plan is final, which is exactly what our free <a href="/free-training/">6 Keys To A Great Home mini-course</a> is built to help with.</p>

<h2>1. Functionality: a layout that works every day</h2>
<p>Everything else rests on this. A great home makes daily routines easy: getting kids out the door, bringing in groceries, doing laundry, working from home. Walk through a normal day on paper before you approve a plan.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Entry and drop zone.</strong> Where do shoes, backpacks, keys and mail land? A mudroom or bench with hooks near the garage door keeps clutter out of the kitchen.</li>
<li><strong>Kitchen workflow.</strong> Keep a clear path between fridge, sink and cooktop, and plan a pantry big enough for how you shop.</li>
<li><strong>Laundry location.</strong> Near the bedrooms is where most of the clothes live.</li>
<li><strong>Storage.</strong> Plan closets, linen storage and garage shelving into the design rather than buying furniture to fix it later.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide on <a href="/how-to-choose-a-floor-plan-for-your-new-home/">how to choose a floor plan for your new home</a> goes deeper. For design inspiration, [[amz:books_pattern_language|A Pattern Language]] is a classic, well-loved book on how spaces support the way people live.</p>

<h2>2. Comfort: a house that feels good to be in</h2>
<p>Comfort starts with things you cannot see. In Texas, that means a home that stays cool and dry without the AC running nonstop: good insulation, tight air sealing, quality windows and a properly sized HVAC system. Read our guide to <a href="/energy-efficient-home-building-in-the-texas-heat/">energy-efficient building in the Texas heat</a> for the details.</p>
<p>Then layer in comfort you can feel:</p>
<ul>
<li>Quiet bedrooms, with insulation in interior walls around bathrooms and the laundry</li>
<li>Layered lighting: overhead, task and warm lamps on dimmers</li>
<li>Soft surfaces such as rugs, curtains and upholstered seating that absorb sound</li>
<li>A thermostat you can control easily, so every room stays comfortable</li>
</ul>
<p>A [[amz:smart_thermostat|smart thermostat]] is a popular way to keep temperatures steady without constant fiddling.</p>

<h2>3. Personal expression: make it unmistakably yours</h2>
<p>A great home tells your story. That does not mean expensive finishes; it means choices that reflect what you love and how you live. A reading nook for the book lover, a wall of family photos in the hallway, a workshop in the garage, a built-in display for travel finds or heirlooms.</p>
<p>When you build, plan for personality early: add blocking in the walls where heavy art or shelves will hang, put outlets where you will want lamps, and leave a few walls simple so your belongings can be the focus. Your home will keep evolving, and that is the point.</p>

<h2>4. Natural light and fresh air</h2>
<p>Daylight makes rooms feel bigger, warmer and more welcoming, and most people simply feel better in a bright home. In Texas, though, light has to be balanced against heat.</p>
<ul>
<li>Favor north and south windows, where light is easier to control.</li>
<li>Shade west-facing glass with porches, overhangs or trees.</li>
<li>Use low-E windows to let in light while limiting heat gain.</li>
<li>Consider clerestory windows or solar tubes to bring daylight into interior spaces.</li>
</ul>
<p>Fresh air matters just as much. Plan operable windows for cross-breezes during mild spring and fall weather, and make sure bathrooms and the kitchen have exhaust fans that vent outside, not into the attic.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Before you finalize window locations, visit the lot in the morning and late afternoon and note where the sun falls. A window that looks perfect on the plan can turn a room into an oven at 5 p.m. in August.</p></div>

<h2>5. Connection to nature</h2>
<p>Texans love being outside, at least when the weather cooperates. A great home blurs the line between indoors and out: a covered patio off the kitchen, a view of trees from the main living area, a garden you can see from the sink.</p>
<ul>
<li>Plan a covered outdoor living space large enough for a table and chairs, ideally on the east or north side for afternoon shade.</li>
<li>Run power, a hose bib and maybe a ceiling fan to the patio during construction.</li>
<li>Save mature trees where you can; they provide shade and character no new planting can match quickly.</li>
<li>Bring nature inside with plants, natural materials like wood and stone, and earthy colors.</li>
</ul>
<p>Even a small herb garden helps. For indoor plants in darker corners, a [[amz:grow_light|compact grow light]] is a simple fix.</p>

<h2>6. Gathering spaces: room to connect</h2>
<p>Homes are where memories happen: holiday meals, game nights, birthday parties and quiet morning coffee. A great home gives people a natural place to come together without feeling crowded.</p>
<ul>
<li>Size the dining area for the biggest gathering you host regularly, not the smallest.</li>
<li>Give the kitchen an island or peninsula where people can sit and talk while you cook.</li>
<li>Balance open space with at least one room that can close off for quiet, homework or a movie.</li>
<li>Think about guest flow: a powder room near the living area, and parking for visitors.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a family of four, this balance between togetherness and privacy is especially important. Our post on <a href="/building-your-texan-haven-choosing-the-ideal-home-for-a-family-of-four/">choosing the ideal home for a family of four</a> shows how different home styles handle it.</p>
